<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balash</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 484–488</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Valakhsh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Balash was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> from 484 to 488, succeeding his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peroz-i">Peroz I</a>, who died fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a>. Facing an exhausted treasury and unrest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, he reached a settlement with the Armenian rebels that granted freedom of Christian worship and restored the Albanian monarchy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vachagan-iii">Vachagan III</a>. He was deposed in 488 in favour of his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavad-i">Kavad I</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balash.jpg" alt="Balash" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_the_Sasanian_king_Balash_from_Susa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
